1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,173
A $100 Bill Costs Pennies.

2
00:00:02,173 --> 00:00:04,013
Who Keeps the Rest?

3
00:00:04,093 --> 00:00:08,733
For 2027, the Federal Reserve has ordered
up to one point three six billion

4
00:00:08,733 --> 00:00:10,720
brand-new hundred-dollar bills.

5
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:11,880
Face value?

6
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:15,840
Up to about one hundred
thirty-seven billion dollars.

7
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:18,960
The cost of the paper, the ink and
the labor for each one?

8
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:20,690
About eleven cents.

9
00:00:21,169 --> 00:00:24,690
So between the eleven cents it costs and
the hundred dollars it's worth,

10
00:00:24,690 --> 00:00:26,209
there are ninety-nine dollars
and eighty-nine cents.

11
00:00:28,289 --> 00:00:30,129
Someone keeps that difference.

12
00:00:30,129 --> 00:00:31,129
Right?

13
00:00:30,929 --> 00:00:31,929
Not exactly.

14
00:00:31,976 --> 00:00:36,776
The real answer is stranger, and it involves
the whole world, a lot of interest,

15
00:00:36,776 --> 00:00:39,896
and a few hundred billion dollars
that haven't been paid.

16
00:00:39,896 --> 00:00:41,976
This is Billions and Bulls.

17
00:00:41,976 --> 00:00:43,865
Let's follow the money.

18
00:00:44,665 --> 00:00:47,225
Part 1 The World's Favorite Bill

19
00:00:47,225 --> 00:00:48,585
Start with a surprise.

20
00:00:48,585 --> 00:00:49,785
Cards are everywhere.

21
00:00:49,785 --> 00:00:51,225
Phones pay for coffee.

22
00:00:51,225 --> 00:00:55,773
And yet, there are more hundred-dollar
bills out there than one-dollar bills.

23
00:00:56,253 --> 00:01:00,493
According to the Federal Reserve, at the end
of 2025 there were about nineteen

24
00:01:00,493 --> 00:01:04,085
point nine billion
hundred-dollar notes in circulation.

25
00:01:04,325 --> 00:01:08,565
And about fifteen point two
billion one-dollar notes.

26
00:01:08,965 --> 00:01:10,850
In value, it isn't close.

27
00:01:11,010 --> 00:01:15,650
Of the roughly two point four trillion
dollars in US cash, almost two trillion

28
00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:16,850
is in hundreds.

29
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:19,490
More than four out of every five dollars.

30
00:01:19,490 --> 00:01:21,203
And the pile keeps growing.

31
00:01:21,203 --> 00:01:24,883
In 2010, there were about
seven billion hundreds.

32
00:01:24,883 --> 00:01:28,643
Fifteen years later, almost
three times as many.

33
00:01:28,723 --> 00:01:31,038
Hundreds also live a long time.

34
00:01:31,038 --> 00:01:35,278
The Fed estimates that a five-dollar bill
lasts less than six years before

35
00:01:35,278 --> 00:01:36,958
it's worn out and destroyed.

36
00:01:36,958 --> 00:01:38,238
A hundred-dollar bill?

37
00:01:38,238 --> 00:01:40,558
About twenty-four years.

38
00:01:40,878 --> 00:01:43,032
That tells you how people use them.

39
00:01:43,192 --> 00:01:44,472
A five gets spent.

40
00:01:44,472 --> 00:01:47,192
It goes from wallet to
register to wallet again.

41
00:01:47,192 --> 00:01:48,792
A hundred mostly sits.

42
00:01:48,792 --> 00:01:51,832
In a safe, under a
mattress, in a bank box.

43
00:01:51,832 --> 00:01:54,312
Or in another country entirely.

44
00:01:54,690 --> 00:01:58,770
The Federal Reserve's own staff estimate
that more than one trillion dollars

45
00:01:58,770 --> 00:02:03,090
in US banknotes were held outside
the country in early 2025.

46
00:02:03,250 --> 00:02:05,730
Roughly half of all the
dollar bills that exist.

47
00:02:05,730 --> 00:02:10,050
For hundreds alone, a Fed working paper
estimated that about seventy percent

48
00:02:10,050 --> 00:02:12,290
were held abroad at the end of 2022.

49
00:02:12,290 --> 00:02:13,970
Nobody knows the exact number.

50
00:02:13,970 --> 00:02:16,761
Once a bill leaves the country,
it can't be tracked.

51
00:02:16,921 --> 00:02:20,921
In places where the local currency can lose
its value fast, a hundred-dollar

52
00:02:20,921 --> 00:02:23,161
bill is a way to keep your savings safe.

53
00:02:23,161 --> 00:02:24,361
It doesn't need a bank.

54
00:02:24,361 --> 00:02:25,562
It doesn't need a phone.

55
00:02:25,562 --> 00:02:27,797
It just needs people to trust it.

56
00:02:28,277 --> 00:02:31,157
The US used to print even bigger bills.

57
00:02:31,157 --> 00:02:34,918
Five hundred, one thousand, five
thousand, even ten thousand dollars.

58
00:02:35,238 --> 00:02:39,321
They were last printed in 1945, and the
government stopped issuing them

59
00:02:39,321 --> 00:02:41,721
in 1969, for lack of use.

60
00:02:41,721 --> 00:02:44,841
Today, the hundred is the
biggest bill the Fed issues.

61
00:02:44,841 --> 00:02:47,059
So, who makes it?

62
00:02:48,018 --> 00:02:50,178
Part 2 Eleven Cents

63
00:02:50,178 --> 00:02:54,019
US paper money is printed by the Bureau
of Engraving and Printing, part

64
00:02:54,019 --> 00:02:55,749
of the Treasury, at two plants.

65
00:02:55,989 --> 00:02:59,189
One in Washington, and one in
Fort Worth, Texas.

66
00:02:59,669 --> 00:03:02,269
But the printer doesn't
decide how much to print.

67
00:03:02,429 --> 00:03:06,269
Every year, the Federal Reserve estimates
how much cash people will need,

68
00:03:06,269 --> 00:03:09,149
at home and abroad, and places an order.

69
00:03:09,149 --> 00:03:11,392
Then it pays the printer for the cost.

70
00:03:11,552 --> 00:03:16,512
For 2027, that order is between four point
eight and five point seven billion

71
00:03:16,512 --> 00:03:18,192
notes, of every kind.

72
00:03:18,192 --> 00:03:20,832
More than a billion of them are hundreds.

73
00:03:21,312 --> 00:03:23,254
The Fed publishes the price list.

74
00:03:23,414 --> 00:03:27,414
In its latest one, for 2025, the
variable cost of printing a one-dollar

75
00:03:27,414 --> 00:03:29,094
bill was about four cents.

76
00:03:29,094 --> 00:03:30,774
A twenty, about seven cents.

77
00:03:30,774 --> 00:03:32,633
And a hundred, eleven point three cents.

78
00:03:32,713 --> 00:03:35,594
The hundred costs more because it
carries more security.

79
00:03:35,594 --> 00:03:39,753
A blue three-D ribbon woven into the
paper, color-shifting ink, a hidden

80
00:03:39,753 --> 00:03:42,073
portrait you can only see
against the light.

81
00:03:42,073 --> 00:03:43,970
Benjamin Franklin is on the front.

82
00:03:43,970 --> 00:03:47,970
The current design went into
circulation in October 2013.

83
00:03:48,850 --> 00:03:51,033
Those prices don't include everything.

84
00:03:51,033 --> 00:03:55,753
On top of the variable costs, the Fed's
2025 budget had almost seven hundred

85
00:03:55,753 --> 00:03:57,804
million dollars for the fixed costs.

86
00:03:58,043 --> 00:04:00,843
The factories, the
engraving, the research.

87
00:04:00,843 --> 00:04:04,843
Even counting all of that, the Fed's
whole currency budget for 2025 was

88
00:04:04,843 --> 00:04:06,443
about one billion dollars.

89
00:04:06,443 --> 00:04:09,804
For a stock of cash worth about
two point four trillion.

90
00:04:10,283 --> 00:04:12,044
Compare that with coins.

91
00:04:12,044 --> 00:04:15,963
Coins are made by a different part of
the Treasury, the US Mint.

92
00:04:15,963 --> 00:04:18,272
And for one coin, the math
went the other way.

93
00:04:18,272 --> 00:04:22,912
In 2024, each penny cost about three
point seven cents to make.

94
00:04:22,912 --> 00:04:26,832
The Mint says that meant a loss of around
eighty-five million dollars in

95
00:04:26,832 --> 00:04:27,832
a single year.

96
00:04:27,716 --> 00:04:32,596
On November 12, 2025, the Mint struck
its last penny for circulation.

97
00:04:33,236 --> 00:04:35,956
The penny lost money every
time it was made.

98
00:04:35,956 --> 00:04:38,078
The hundred-dollar bill looks
like the opposite.

99
00:04:38,238 --> 00:04:40,398
Eleven cents in, a hundred dollars out.

100
00:04:40,398 --> 00:04:43,759
So surely, someone is pocketing
ninety-nine dollars and eighty-nine cents.

101
00:04:45,438 --> 00:04:47,198
Let's find them.

102
00:04:48,158 --> 00:04:51,278
Part 3 Nobody Keeps $99.89

103
00:04:52,159 --> 00:04:53,358
Here's the twist.

104
00:04:53,358 --> 00:04:57,598
When a new hundred-dollar bill leaves the
Fed, nobody gets ninety-nine dollars

105
00:04:57,598 --> 00:04:58,920
and eighty-nine cents.

106
00:04:59,160 --> 00:05:02,760
Not the Fed, not the printer,
not the government.

107
00:05:03,560 --> 00:05:05,640
Here's how cash really gets to you.

108
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:08,200
Your bank needs more bills for its ATMs.

109
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:10,047
It asks the Federal Reserve for them.

110
00:05:10,206 --> 00:05:13,966
The Fed sends the cash, and takes the same
amount out of the bank's account

111
00:05:13,966 --> 00:05:14,966
at the Fed.

112
00:05:14,846 --> 00:05:18,446
One hundred dollars of cash out, one
hundred dollars of account balance

113
00:05:18,446 --> 00:05:19,446
gone.

114
00:05:19,086 --> 00:05:20,920
The bank didn't get anything for free.

115
00:05:20,920 --> 00:05:24,120
It swapped one kind of money for another.

116
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:27,400
On the Fed's books, that
bill is not an asset.

117
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:28,690
It's a liability.

118
00:05:28,850 --> 00:05:32,610
It's basically an IOU from the Federal
Reserve that you can carry around

119
00:05:32,610 --> 00:05:33,610
in your wallet.

120
00:05:33,570 --> 00:05:35,970
And it has one very special feature.

121
00:05:35,970 --> 00:05:37,410
It pays no interest.

122
00:05:37,410 --> 00:05:38,610
Ever.

123
00:05:39,090 --> 00:05:40,514
Think about your own cash.

124
00:05:40,514 --> 00:05:43,873
Say you keep ten hundred-dollar
bills in a drawer for a year.

125
00:05:43,873 --> 00:05:48,113
If a savings account pays three percent, you
just gave up about thirty dollars

126
00:05:48,113 --> 00:05:49,113
of interest.

127
00:05:48,876 --> 00:05:50,716
You didn't lose the thousand dollars.

128
00:05:50,716 --> 00:05:53,036
But somebody else earned
those thirty dollars.

129
00:05:53,036 --> 00:05:54,236
Who?

130
00:05:54,556 --> 00:05:56,797
Now look at the other side
of the Fed's books.

131
00:05:56,797 --> 00:05:59,916
To balance all that cash,
the Fed holds assets.

132
00:05:59,916 --> 00:06:01,916
Mostly US government bonds.

133
00:06:01,916 --> 00:06:04,716
And those bonds do pay interest.

134
00:06:04,956 --> 00:06:06,187
So here's the trick.

135
00:06:06,347 --> 00:06:09,227
The Fed owes you a piece of
paper that pays nothing.

136
00:06:09,227 --> 00:06:11,627
And it holds a bond that pays something.

137
00:06:11,627 --> 00:06:15,067
The gap between the two is the real
profit from printing money.

138
00:06:15,067 --> 00:06:17,054
Economists call it seigniorage.

139
00:06:17,214 --> 00:06:20,894
The word comes from the old French for
lord, the seigneur, who used to keep

140
00:06:20,894 --> 00:06:23,294
a cut when he minted coins.

141
00:06:23,774 --> 00:06:25,214
How big can it get?

142
00:06:25,214 --> 00:06:28,015
Some simple math, just to
get a feel for it.

143
00:06:28,335 --> 00:06:30,735
Two point four trillion dollars of cash.

144
00:06:30,735 --> 00:06:34,735
If the bonds behind it earn three percent a
year, that's about seventy billion

145
00:06:34,735 --> 00:06:35,735
dollars a year.

146
00:06:35,695 --> 00:06:37,055
Not from printing.

147
00:06:37,055 --> 00:06:41,612
From the interest that cash holders
give up, every single day.

148
00:06:41,692 --> 00:06:46,252
If you're enjoying the story so far,
subscribing helps the channel a lot.

149
00:06:46,252 --> 00:06:50,227
Because now we follow that
interest, and see who gets it.

150
00:06:51,106 --> 00:06:53,826
Part 4 Where the Profit Goes

151
00:06:53,826 --> 00:06:56,066
The Federal Reserve isn't
a normal company.

152
00:06:56,066 --> 00:06:59,939
By law, after it pays its costs and a
small dividend to its member banks,

153
00:07:00,099 --> 00:07:03,059
the rest of its profit goes
to the US Treasury.

154
00:07:03,619 --> 00:07:05,860
In good years, that's a lot of money.

155
00:07:05,860 --> 00:07:11,299
In 2021, the Fed sent the Treasury one
hundred seven point four billion

156
00:07:11,299 --> 00:07:12,299
dollars.

157
00:07:11,596 --> 00:07:15,837
That same year, producing, issuing and
retiring all of the country's paper

158
00:07:15,837 --> 00:07:19,836
money cost the Reserve Banks
about one billion dollars.

159
00:07:19,836 --> 00:07:22,602
Roughly one percent of what they
sent to the Treasury.

160
00:07:22,761 --> 00:07:25,161
Not all of that came from cash, of course.

161
00:07:25,161 --> 00:07:28,921
The Fed also earns interest on bonds
it bought with bank reserves.

162
00:07:28,921 --> 00:07:32,941
But cash is the part that has
always cost the Fed nothing.

163
00:07:33,421 --> 00:07:36,541
So the first answer to our
question is the US government.

164
00:07:36,541 --> 00:07:39,341
And through the government,
American taxpayers.

165
00:07:39,341 --> 00:07:42,941
Every dollar the Fed sends to the Treasury
is a dollar the government doesn't

166
00:07:42,941 --> 00:07:44,941
have to raise in taxes or borrow.

167
00:07:46,141 --> 00:07:48,701
But follow the bills one more step.

168
00:07:48,701 --> 00:07:51,684
Remember the trillion dollars
of US cash held abroad?

169
00:07:51,923 --> 00:07:56,323
Every person outside the US who keeps
savings in hundred-dollar bills is,

170
00:07:56,323 --> 00:07:59,043
in effect, lending money to
the United States.

171
00:07:59,043 --> 00:08:00,323
At zero interest.

172
00:08:00,323 --> 00:08:01,477
With no end date.

173
00:08:01,477 --> 00:08:06,037
It's one of the quieter advantages of
having the world's favorite currency.

174
00:08:06,597 --> 00:08:09,568
The Fed's own staff noticed
something about that, too.

175
00:08:09,807 --> 00:08:13,807
They say foreign demand for dollar bills
has slowed in the last couple of

176
00:08:13,807 --> 00:08:14,807
years.

177
00:08:14,448 --> 00:08:17,510
One reason, they say, is
higher interest rates.

178
00:08:17,510 --> 00:08:21,749
When a bank account pays real interest,
keeping a stack of bills in a drawer

179
00:08:21,749 --> 00:08:22,950
costs you more.

180
00:08:22,950 --> 00:08:24,552
And there's a new competitor.

181
00:08:24,552 --> 00:08:29,512
The Fed's staff say digital tokens tied to
the dollar, called stablecoins,

182
00:08:29,512 --> 00:08:33,881
seem to be replacing paper dollars for
some people in developing countries.

183
00:08:34,361 --> 00:08:37,161
And that's the hidden price
of holding cash.

184
00:08:37,161 --> 00:08:40,921
You give up the interest, and inflation
slowly eats away at what the bill

185
00:08:40,921 --> 00:08:41,921
can buy.

186
00:08:41,721 --> 00:08:43,888
Someone else earns that interest instead.

187
00:08:44,128 --> 00:08:48,608
For most of the last century, that someone
was the Fed, and then the Treasury.

188
00:08:48,608 --> 00:08:51,088
Until it suddenly wasn't.

189
00:08:52,368 --> 00:08:55,145
Part 5 When Printing Money Lost Money

190
00:08:55,385 --> 00:09:00,345
In 2022, inflation surged, and the Fed
raised interest rates fast.

191
00:09:00,345 --> 00:09:02,665
That changed the math completely.

192
00:09:03,305 --> 00:09:05,516
Remember, the Fed doesn't only owe cash.

193
00:09:05,675 --> 00:09:09,355
It also owes banks the reserves sitting
in their accounts at the Fed.

194
00:09:09,355 --> 00:09:13,435
And since 2008, the Fed has paid
interest on those reserves.

195
00:09:13,435 --> 00:09:17,599
During the pandemic, the Fed bought
trillions of dollars of bonds, paying

196
00:09:17,599 --> 00:09:19,199
for them with new reserves.

197
00:09:19,199 --> 00:09:23,588
By 2022, its balance sheet was close
to nine trillion dollars.

198
00:09:23,828 --> 00:09:27,028
Almost four times all the US
cash in circulation.

199
00:09:27,028 --> 00:09:29,348
When rates were near zero, that was cheap.

200
00:09:29,348 --> 00:09:33,508
When rates went above five percent,
it became very expensive.

201
00:09:34,088 --> 00:09:37,448
Meanwhile, many of the bonds the Fed owned
had been bought when rates were

202
00:09:37,448 --> 00:09:38,448
low.

203
00:09:37,928 --> 00:09:39,768
They kept paying low interest.

204
00:09:39,768 --> 00:09:43,369
So the Fed was paying more on what it
owed than it was earning on what it

205
00:09:43,369 --> 00:09:44,369
owned.

206
00:09:44,733 --> 00:09:49,533
In September 2022, most of the regional
Federal Reserve Banks stopped sending

207
00:09:49,533 --> 00:09:50,893
money to the Treasury.

208
00:09:50,893 --> 00:09:54,802
Instead, they started recording
what's called a deferred asset.

209
00:09:54,802 --> 00:09:58,802
It's basically a running total of the
losses they have to earn back before

210
00:09:58,802 --> 00:10:01,122
payments can start again.

211
00:10:01,362 --> 00:10:06,512
By June 2026, that total was about two
hundred thirty-five billion dollars.

212
00:10:06,512 --> 00:10:10,592
The Fed says it doesn't affect how it
runs monetary policy, and it's not

213
00:10:10,592 --> 00:10:11,950
part of the national debt.

214
00:10:12,109 --> 00:10:15,709
But it means the profit from all that
cash, the two trillion dollars of

215
00:10:15,709 --> 00:10:18,749
interest-free money, is going
to fill a hole first.

216
00:10:18,749 --> 00:10:22,029
Some of the regional Fed banks have
started paying the Treasury again.

217
00:10:22,029 --> 00:10:23,803
Most have not.

218
00:10:24,282 --> 00:10:26,442
It's not just an American story.

219
00:10:26,442 --> 00:10:30,283
In Germany, the Bundesbank reported a
loss of nineteen point two billion

220
00:10:30,283 --> 00:10:32,042
euros for 2024.

221
00:10:32,042 --> 00:10:34,374
Its first loss since 1979.

222
00:10:34,534 --> 00:10:37,894
In 2025, it lost another
eight point six billion.

223
00:10:37,894 --> 00:10:41,334
For years, the German government had
reportedly counted on about two and

224
00:10:41,334 --> 00:10:44,214
a half billion euros a year
from its central bank.

225
00:10:44,214 --> 00:10:45,991
That money stopped arriving.

226
00:10:45,991 --> 00:10:51,670
In 2019, the euro area also stopped issuing
its biggest note, the five-hundred-euro

227
00:10:51,670 --> 00:10:52,670
bill.

228
00:10:52,230 --> 00:10:55,911
The European Central Bank said it was
concerned the note could make illegal

229
00:10:55,911 --> 00:10:57,670
activity easier.

230
00:10:58,823 --> 00:11:01,223
Part 6 So, Who Keeps the Rest?

231
00:11:01,223 --> 00:11:05,063
So let's follow the
hundred-dollar bill one last time.

232
00:11:05,143 --> 00:11:08,655
It costs about eleven cents in
paper, ink and labor.

233
00:11:08,894 --> 00:11:12,494
Nobody pockets the other ninety-nine
dollars and eighty-nine cents on the

234
00:11:12,494 --> 00:11:13,694
day it's printed.

235
00:11:13,694 --> 00:11:16,708
The money comes later,
slowly, as interest.

236
00:11:16,868 --> 00:11:21,189
The Fed holds a bond that pays interest,
while you hold a bill that doesn't.

237
00:11:21,189 --> 00:11:24,788
In normal times, that profit
goes to the US Treasury.

238
00:11:25,348 --> 00:11:26,788
And who pays for it?

239
00:11:26,788 --> 00:11:28,253
Everyone who holds cash.

240
00:11:28,493 --> 00:11:32,493
People here, and maybe more than a trillion
dollars' worth of savers abroad,

241
00:11:32,493 --> 00:11:34,784
giving up interest to keep
money they can touch.

242
00:11:34,943 --> 00:11:38,943
Right now, that profit is quietly paying
back the Fed's losses before the

243
00:11:38,943 --> 00:11:41,663
Treasury sees much of it again.

244
00:11:41,744 --> 00:11:44,274
Not everyone thinks big
bills are a good idea.

245
00:11:44,434 --> 00:11:48,354
The Harvard economist Kenneth Rogoff has
argued that large notes like the

246
00:11:48,354 --> 00:11:52,514
hundred make tax evasion and crime
easier, and that they should be phased

247
00:11:52,514 --> 00:11:53,514
out.

248
00:11:53,075 --> 00:11:57,441
Others say cash protects privacy, works
when the power goes out, and is

249
00:11:57,441 --> 00:12:01,362
a lifeline for people without a bank, or
in countries where the bank can't

250
00:12:01,362 --> 00:12:02,801
be trusted.

251
00:12:03,121 --> 00:12:06,693
A hundred-dollar bill isn't worth a
hundred dollars because of the paper.

252
00:12:06,693 --> 00:12:09,973
It's worth a hundred dollars because
people all over the world agree to

253
00:12:09,973 --> 00:12:10,973
hold it.

254
00:12:10,613 --> 00:12:14,307
And while they hold it, someone
else collects the interest.

